Derive uniform magnetic motion without assigning work to deflection (@physics-magnetic-orbit: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Connect-steady-currents-to-magnetic-geometry-and-its-limits.lingua#r_005AC3749D945400D7238C408B") { r_00790A783DA8CDBA03CB1FFFF4
Take a constant nonzero B=B e_z, E=0, constant mass m>0 and signed charge q≠0. Assume nonrelativistic motion, no other forces and negligible radiation reaction; this is a prescribed external-field model. Let ω=qB/m retain its sign. Then v̇_x=ωv_y, v̇_y=−ωv_x and v̇_z=0. With c=cos(ωt),s=sin(ωt), the solution is v_x=v_x0 c+v_y0 s, v_y=v_y0 c−v_x0 s. The existing trigonometric derivatives verify the equation and initial values.

For uniqueness, subtract two solutions with the same initial state. The derivative of the squared velocity difference is 2ω(δv_xδv_y−δv_yδv_x)=0, so it remains zero. Positions then agree by integrating velocity. Integrating the solution gives x=x₀+[v_x0 s+v_y0(1−c)]/ω, y=y₀+[v_y0 s−v_x0(1−c)]/ω, z=z₀+v_z0 t. The center (x+v_y/ω,y−v_x/ω) stays fixed. The perpendicular radius is v_perp/|ω|; parallel motion gives a helix. If qB=0, use straight motion rather than divide by zero.

The power identity is v·q(v×B)=0. Thus kinetic energy is constant in this model, even though the force is nonzero. With E included, d(mv²/2)/dt=qE·v. A classical gyrating charge is not a stable atomic state; compare the premises with [Review quantum descriptions with their physical premises and evidence limits](Review-quantum-descriptions-with-their-physical-premises-and-evidence-limits.lingua#r_00CFA9ABD5C9D7D59E8532DC9F). A field that changes appreciably over an orbit, a relativistic speed or appreciable radiation requires another model.

Use [Investigate periodic states exact rotations and sampling ambiguity](../../mathematics/core/Investigate-periodic-states-exact-rotations-and-sampling-ambiguity.lingua#r_001D348AE25C32431381761409) for the unchanged exact complex multiplication that represents a planar rotation. In normalized units, m=2,q=1,B=3, position (0,0) and velocity (3,4), evaluate the phase with cos=3/5,sin=4/5. The velocity becomes (5,0), position (8/3,4/3), center (8/3,−2), and kinetic energy remains 25. The supplied rational phase identifies points on the analytic trajectory; it does not assert a rational elapsed time or approximate time stepping.

**Work:** verify the trajectory by differentiation, not just by constant speed. For positive B and charge, a particle initially moving along +x bends toward −y. Reverse q and compare the orbit orientation at the same positive time; the signed sine changes too. Show why taking |q| in the differential equations erases physical information. For crossed uniform fields, derive E=−v×B for an undeflected velocity and identify the velocity component that this equation leaves unconstrained.

**Continue:** [Connect steady currents to magnetic geometry and its limits](Connect-steady-currents-to-magnetic-geometry-and-its-limits.lingua#r_005AC3749D945400D7238C408B).

